  "worked_solution": "First, we apply the sum rule and write our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[0,1]\\times [0,2]\\times [-1,1]$ is a rectangular box, and each integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our sum of repeated integrals as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z\\,+\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate each of these separately:\n\n- For the first product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z & =(1-0)\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{16}{3}=\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
